      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\), we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\). Here's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Identify the given angles in the triangle \\(ABC\\):\n   - \\(\\angle A = 93^\\circ\\)\n   - \\(\\angle CBD = 130^\\circ\\)\n\n2. Recognize that \\(\\angle CBD\\) is an exterior angle to \\(\\triangle ABC\\) at vertex \\(C\\). The measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. Therefore, \\(\\angle CBD = \\angle BAC + \\angle ACB\\).\n\n3. Substitute the known values into the equation:\n   \\[\n   130^\\circ = 93^\\circ + \\angle ACB\n   \\]\n\n4. Solve for \\(\\angle ACB\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle ACB = 130^\\circ - 93^\\circ = 37^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is \\(\\boxed{37}\\).",
